Module 02 – Linux, Networking & Cloud Infrastructure

1. Linux Fundamentals

Linux is an open-source operating system widely used in servers, cloud infrastructure and development environments.

Command-line navigation

Command	Purpose
pwd	Displays the current working directory.
ls	Lists files and directories.
ls -la	Displays hidden files and detailed file information.
cd	Changes the current directory.
mkdir	Creates a directory.
touch	Creates an empty file.
cat	Displays file contents.
cp	Copies files or directories.
mv	Moves or renames files.
rm	Removes files.
rmdir	Removes an empty directory.

File permissions

Linux permissions determine who can read, write or execute a file.

There are three permission categories:

* User (u): The file owner.
* Group (g): Users belonging to the file’s group.
* Others (o): Everyone else.

Permission values:

Permission	Symbol	Numeric value
Read	r	4
Write	w	2
Execute	x	1

Examples:

* chmod 755 script.sh – owner gets full permissions; group and others get read and execute.
* chmod 644 notes.txt – owner can read and write; group and others can read.
* chmod u+x script.sh – adds execute permission for the owner.
* ls -l – displays file permissions and ownership.

Users and groups

Linux uses users and groups to manage access to system resources.

Commands explored:

* whoami – displays the current username.
* id – displays user and group IDs.
* groups – lists the groups a user belongs to.
* getent group – retrieves group information.
* sudo – executes commands with elevated privileges.
* chown – changes file or directory ownership.

A shared directory was configured for a development group. The setgid permission (chmod 2770) was used so new files created inside the directory inherit its group ownership.

Process management

A process is a running instance of a program.

Commands explored:

* ps – displays processes.
* ps aux – displays a detailed process list.
* top – monitors running processes and resource usage.
* pgrep – searches for processes by name.
* kill – sends a signal to a process.
* jobs – lists jobs started in the current shell.

A background process was started using sleep 300 &, inspected and terminated.

Service management

A Linux service is a background program that provides functionality such as SSH access or web hosting.

systemd is a service and system manager. systemctl is the command-line tool used to interact with it.

Commands explored:

* systemctl status ssh – checks the SSH service status.
* sudo systemctl start ssh – starts SSH.
* sudo systemctl stop ssh – stops SSH.
* sudo systemctl restart ssh – restarts SSH.
* sudo systemctl enable ssh – enables SSH at startup.
* systemctl is-enabled ssh – checks whether SSH is enabled.

2. Networking Fundamentals

Networking allows computers and servers to communicate and exchange data.

IP addressing

An IP address identifies a device or network interface.

Two common versions are:

* IPv4: Uses 32-bit addresses, for example 192.168.1.10.
* IPv6: Uses 128-bit addresses, allowing a much larger address space.

Commands explored:

* ip addr – displays network interfaces and IP addresses.
* ip -4 addr – displays IPv4 addresses.
* ping -c 4 8.8.8.8 – sends four connectivity tests to an IP address.

The Ubuntu WSL2 environment displayed a private IPv4 address on its virtual network interface.

DNS

DNS stands for Domain Name System. It translates domain names, such as google.com, into IP addresses that computers can use to communicate.

Commands explored:

* getent hosts google.com – checks whether a hostname can be resolved.
* cat /etc/resolv.conf – displays DNS resolver configuration.

The DNS lookup successfully returned an IP address for google.com.

The resolver configuration indicated that the file was automatically generated by WSL.

Ports and protocols

A port identifies a communication endpoint used by a service.

Port	Protocol/service	Purpose
22	SSH	Secure remote access
53	DNS	Domain name resolution
80	HTTP	Web traffic
443	HTTPS	Encrypted web traffic

TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) provides reliable, ordered data delivery.

UDP (User Datagram Protocol) provides connectionless communication with lower overhead.

Examples:

* SSH normally uses TCP port 22.
* HTTP normally uses TCP port 80.
* HTTPS normally uses TCP port 443.
* DNS commonly uses UDP port 53 and can also use TCP port 53.

Commands explored:

* ss -tulpn – displays listening sockets and associated processes.
* curl -I http://localhost – sends an HTTP request and displays response headers.

The practical environment showed SSH listening on port 22 and Nginx listening on port 80.

Firewalls

A firewall controls network traffic according to defined security rules.

It can restrict which connections are allowed or denied based on factors such as source, destination, port and protocol.

Tools explored conceptually:

* UFW: Uncomplicated Firewall, a Linux firewall management tool.
* nftables: A Linux framework for configuring packet filtering.

UFW and nftables were not available in the Ubuntu environment during the practical checks. No firewall rules were changed or claimed as configured.

AWS Security Groups

An AWS Security Group acts as a virtual firewall associated with supported AWS resources, such as EC2 instances.

Security Groups control traffic through:

* Inbound rules: Define which incoming connections are allowed.
* Outbound rules: Define which outgoing connections are allowed.
* Protocol: For example TCP or UDP.
* Port range: For example TCP port 22 or 80.
* Source/destination: Defines where traffic may come from or go to.

Example of typical inbound rules for a web server:

Type	Protocol	Port	Example source
SSH	TCP	22	Trusted administrator IP
HTTP	TCP	80	0.0.0.0/0
HTTPS	TCP	443	0.0.0.0/0

Security best practice: restrict SSH access to trusted IP addresses instead of exposing port 22 to everyone.

No AWS Security Group was created or modified for this exercise.

3. SSH Configuration and Secure Access

SSH stands for Secure Shell. It is a protocol used to securely access and manage remote computers over a network.

SSH key authentication

SSH supports public-key authentication using a key pair:

* Private key: Kept secret by the client.
* Public key: Installed on the server in the user’s authorized_keys file.

An Ed25519 key pair was generated from Windows PowerShell:

ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "module2-devops"

The public key was added to Ubuntu’s:

~/.ssh/authorized_keys

Permissions were configured:

* .ssh directory: 700
* authorized_keys: 600

The SSH server’s effective configuration was checked and public-key authentication was enabled.

Connection test

A connection was established from Windows PowerShell to Ubuntu using:

ssh -i "$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519" zee_zol@localhost

The connection was successful, and whoami returned zee_zol.

The private key remained on Windows. Only the public key was installed in Ubuntu.

4. Linux Server Deployment – Nginx

Nginx is a web server that handles HTTP requests and delivers web content to clients. It can also be used as a reverse proxy and load balancer.

Installation

The following commands were used:

sudo apt update

Updates the package information available to Ubuntu.

sudo apt install nginx

Installs the Nginx web server.

Service verification

systemctl status nginx

Checks whether Nginx is running.

sudo systemctl enable nginx

Enables Nginx to start automatically when the system starts.

Connectivity testing

Nginx was verified as listening on TCP port 80.

The service was accessed from the Windows browser using:

http://localhost

The Nginx welcome page was displayed successfully.

This confirmed that the Windows host could access the web service running in the Ubuntu WSL2 environment.

5. Practical Environment and Limitations

The practical exercises were completed using:

* Windows 11 as the host operating system.
* Ubuntu running under Windows Subsystem for Linux 2 (WSL2).
* OpenSSH for secure access.
* Nginx for web server deployment.

WSL2 provides a Linux environment backed by a lightweight virtual machine. It is not equivalent to independently creating and managing a standalone virtual machine using VirtualBox, Hyper-V or a cloud provider.

The brief did not specify a VM platform. No standalone VM or AWS EC2 instance was provisioned.

No AWS infrastructure was deployed, and no cloud resources were intentionally created for this practical exercise.

